{"text":"Recent work supports combining collaborative IDs with item text, but cautions against naive fusion. [TedRec (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166) fuses text and ID sequences through frequency-domain operations; its implementation uses pretrained BERT, so its results do not establish effectiveness for scratch-trained text encoders. [MARS (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.00702) uses attribute-aware encoding and matching to capture multiple user interests, again relying on pretrained language representations. [MiFuSR (2026)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126005046) identifies modality distortion and noise propagation as problems with invasive fusion, motivating preservation of the collaborative representation.

Evidence specifically supporting title/description encoders trained from scratch on sparse Amazon interactions remains limited in the retrieved literature. Pretrained-text gains should therefore be treated as architectural motivation, not expected benchmark improvements. [WhitenRec (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10602) additionally shows that text representations can be insufficiently discriminative: semantic similarity alone need not distinguish recommendation targets. None of these findings establishes that similar wording implies equipment compatibility, format substitutability, or a valid next-item transition.

A task-compatible hypothesis is to retain SASRec’s ID pathway and add a small, randomly initialized token encoder trained jointly through next-item prediction. Encode titles and descriptions separately; mask absent fields and learn a bounded residual gate so text can contribute selectively. Compare against ID-only SASRec and simple score fusion, inspecting missing-text and item-frequency slices. These are proposed adaptations, not published guarantees; select using the prescribed macro validation HR@10 and use only observed text and training interactions."}
